course: english
lesson: 36
title: Brackets, dashes, colons, semicolons and hyphens
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Brackets, dashes and commas can all hold extra information that you could lift out and still leave a full sentence: "My dog (a spaniel) loves walks" still works as "My dog loves walks"; this extra part is called parenthesis.
explain: Brackets whisper the extra bit, dashes say it louder and add drama ("My dog – a spaniel – loves walks"), and an ellipsis of three dots makes the reader wait... or shows that words are missing.
explain: A colon is a drum roll after a complete sentence: something is coming, a list or an explanation ("You need three things: pens, paper and glue").
explain: A semicolon is a bridge between two full sentences that belong together ("It rained; we stayed in"), and it can also sort a long list whose items already contain commas.
explain: A hyphen is a short join inside words, and it can change the story: a "man-eating shark" eats people, but a "man eating shark" is a man having shark for dinner, as a Cardiff gran might put it, "isn't it, cariad?" ("cariad" is Welsh for love or darling).
fact: Parenthesis is extra information added to a sentence; the sentence still makes sense if you take it out.
fact: Brackets, a pair of dashes, or a pair of commas can all mark parenthesis.
fact: Dashes give a stronger break than brackets or commas, and can make writing more dramatic; used too often they lose their effect.
fact: A dash is longer than a hyphen and usually has a space on each side; a hyphen is short and has no spaces.
fact: A colon comes after a complete sentence and introduces a list, an explanation or an example.
fact: A semicolon joins two complete sentences that are closely related: "It rained; we stayed in."
fact: A semicolon can separate items in a long list when the items already contain commas: "Paris, France; Rome, Italy; and Madrid, Spain."
fact: A hyphen joins words to make one meaning, especially describing words before a noun: a man-eating shark, a well-known song.
fact: Some hyphens stop two different words from looking the same: re-cover (cover again) and recover (get better).
fact: In British slang "well" means very, as in "I'm well tired"; no hyphen is needed there.
fact: An ellipsis is exactly three dots; it shows suspense, a trailing-off voice, or missing words.
fact: In Australian English "heaps" means lots or very, as in "heaps of dashes".
exercise: Year 5: "My dog (a spaniel) loves walks." What do the brackets hold? | Extra information that could be removed | The most important word of the sentence | A secret the dog is keeping | A list of things the dog likes
exercise: Jack writes "My dog – a spaniel – loves walks." What job do his dashes do? | The same parenthesis job as the brackets | They join two separate words into one single word | They show that letters are missing | They start a list of dog breeds
exercise: Which is the dash, not the hyphen? | The longer one with spaces round it | The short one inside one word | The one that looks like a tiny raised dot | The curly one above the letters
exercise: Where does the colon go: "You need pens, paper, glue"? | After "need" | After "pens" | After "glue" | Before "You"
exercise: Year 6: "It rained; we stayed in." Why is a semicolon used? | It joins two closely related complete sentences | It shows the end of the whole story | It starts a list of weather words | It hides extra information from the reader
exercise: Which sentence uses a semicolon correctly? | The bell rang; we ran out. | The bell; rang we ran out. | The bell rang we; ran out. | ; The bell rang we ran out.
exercise: Gran from Cardiff asks "A hyphen's for 'man-eating shark', isn't it, cariad?" What does the hyphen do? | It shows the shark eats people | It shows a man is eating a shark | It shows there are two sharks | It means the shark is in brackets
exercise: Sam writes "I'm well-tired." His teacher says no hyphen is needed. Why? | "Well" is slang for very, not a joined describing word | Hyphens are banned in every kind of slang writing | "Tired" never goes next to a hyphen | It should be a dash instead of a hyphen
exercise: Year 6: insert a colon in "I know what to do pack, leave, run." | I know what to do: pack, leave, run. | I know: what to do pack, leave, run. | I know what to: do pack, leave, run. | I know what to do pack: leave, run.
exercise: Kai asks "Can semicolons go in lists?" What is the answer? | Yes, in long lists whose items already have commas | No, they can only ever join two sentences | Yes, between every single word of a list | No, lists can only use full stops
exercise: An Aussie kid says "Heaps of dashes in this book." His teacher says "They make it dramatic." What does "heaps" mean? | Lots | A few | Piles of rubbish | Almost none
exercise: How many dots are in an ellipsis? | Three | Two | Four | As many as you like
exercise: What can an ellipsis show? | Suspense or missing words | A list of three things | The end of a question | Exactly where a hyphen goes
exercise: Which pair of words differs only by a hyphen and means something different? | re-cover and recover | re-cover and uncover | cover and covers | recovered and recover
exercise: Which punctuation can mark parenthesis? | Brackets, dashes or commas | Only full stops and colons | Only question marks | Only hyphens and dots
exercise: In which sentence is the colon wrong, because the words before it are not a complete sentence? | Bring: a coat and boots. | Bring these: a coat and boots. | You need two things: a coat and boots. | Pack the list: a coat and boots.
test: Your mate goes "brackets are for stuff that doesn't matter, so the teacher skips it." What is truer? | It is extra detail that the sentence can stand without | Teachers never read anything in brackets | Brackets mean the words inside them are spelled wrong | Brackets turn a sentence into a question
test: ★★★ "A little used car" and "a little-used car." Which one is a car that is not driven much? | A little-used car | A little used car | Both mean exactly that | Neither means that
test: In a cartoon, a villain's note says "Meet me at the dock... alone." Why the three dots? | They build suspense before "alone" | They show the villain forgot a word | They are decoration, they mean nothing | They show that "alone" is a list
test: What would happen if the hyphen fell out of "man-eating shark"? | It could mean a man eating a shark | It would still mean exactly the same | It would mean a shark eating a man's dinner | It would mean the shark is a man
test: ★★★ Which sentence uses a semicolon wrongly? | We went to the beach; because it was hot. | We went to the beach; it was hot. | It was late; the shops had shut. | Mum laughed; Dad didn't.
test: A Gen Z cousin texts "the vibe — immaculate." Which mark is she using to add drama? | A dash | A hyphen | A colon | A semicolon
test: Nan in Belfast, dry: "You need three things, love: coat, hat, and stop moaning." What does the colon do? | It introduces the list after a full clause | It joins two words into one word | It shows missing letters in "love" | It ends Nan's question
test: ★★★ Choose the best punctuation: "We visited Leeds, England ___ Cork, Ireland ___ and Perth, Australia." | Semicolons in both gaps | Commas in both gaps | Colons in both gaps | Hyphens in both gaps
test: Your brother writes "I like my teacher – she is funny – she is kind – she is fair – she is tall." What would a good editor say? | Too many dashes lose their effect | It needs even more dashes to be clear | Dashes can never be used in a list | It is perfect, dashes are always best
test: A Liverpool lad says "The match – our kid's first – was boss." ("Boss" means great.) Lift out the part between the dashes. What is left? | The match was boss. | Our kid's first was boss. | The match our kid's first. | Was boss.
test: Which shows the sofa was covered again, not that someone got better? | re-covered | recovered | re covered | re: covered
exam: Identify the sentence in which a colon is correctly employed to introduce a list. | The recipe requires three ingredients: flour, eggs and milk. | The recipe: requires three ingredients flour, eggs and milk. | The recipe requires: three ingredients flour, eggs and milk. | The recipe requires three: ingredients flour, eggs and milk.
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of the semicolon in "It rained; we stayed in"? | It links two independent clauses closely related in meaning | It introduces a list of items subsequently enumerated | It denotes the omission of letters | It marks the end of a question
exam: Select the response consistent with established convention concerning parenthesis. | It may be indicated by brackets, dashes or commas | It may be indicated solely by brackets | It must contain the main verb of the sentence | It may not be removed without loss of grammaticality
exam: Ascertain the number of full stops constituting an ellipsis. | three | two | four | seven
